## Authority this case is graded against

* **X960 platform support TPS**, fetched 2026-10-20:

      TPS row 275 | Management | TACACS+ | Accounting | Priority 1 | Supported: Yes

  => **declared supported**, so an absent or incorrect behaviour is a FINDING, not a platform
  limit.

* **"TACACS+ AAA" TFS**, project 1599
  (`https://wiki.atlnz.lc/awpwiki/index.php/TACACS%2B_AAA_TFS`).  This case is graded against
  seventeen of its specifications, listed with their results in the summary table at the end.
  The ones that carry the case:
  - **S1599.1.022** no `tacacs+` option => no login accounting message is sent;
  - **S1599.1.023 / .024** a START on login, a STOP on logout;
  - **S1599.1.025** login accounting is **global**, with the same behaviour on a console and
    on a VTY session;
  - **S1599.1.026** configuration changes apply only to users **when they log in**; users
    already logged in retain the configuration that existed when they logged in;
  - **S1599.1.027 / .028** `task_id` increments sequentially per session, and the STOP's
    `task_id` is identical to its START's;
  - **S1599.1.030 - .046** command accounting: per-privilege selection (.031), a record for
    each accepted and executed command (.033), **no command output** (.034), **abbreviations
    expanded** (.036), config-mode commands at privilege 15 (.041), `do` commands at privilege
    1 and **not** expanded (.042), a unique `task_id` per command (.043), and **mutual
    exclusion with triggers in both directions** (.045 / .046);
  - **S1599.1.050 / .052 / .054** periodic updates, whose `task_id` must be **identical to the
    START and STOP of the same session**;
  - **S1599.2.06 - .09** deliberately NO record for `enable`, an incomplete command, `?`, an
    invalid command, or a command that did not execute.

## Objective (designed by the tester)

Prove that this DUT reports CLI activity to an external TACACS+ accounting server: that a
login and a logout produce correctly paired records, that each configured mode (`start-stop`,
`stop-only`, `none`) produces exactly what it says, that command accounting records the right
commands with the right privilege and the right text, and that each record can be attributed
to the session that caused it.  Then prove the feature can be removed and leaves nothing
behind.

**Design note -- why this case is risk-free where T33411 was not.**  Accounting is independent
of authentication (the TFS says so in as many words: *"User login accounting is separate from
user login authentication. When a user logs in, regardless of what authentication method was
used, the user login accounting will behave as per its configuration."*).  So this case leaves
`aaa authentication login default local` **untouched** throughout.  `manager` therefore keeps
working on every path, the x960 §7E.1 lockout trap never applies, and no escape account is
needed.  It also means every accounting record below was produced for a **locally
authenticated** login -- which is itself the measurement of S1599.1.025's independence claim.

