## Instruments

The same three as T33411 (see that log for the full description), but this case leans on the
second one:

1. **The DUT** -- `show running-config`, `show users`, `show trigger`, `show log`; console
   driven through `tools/tb` with `ckcon.py`, `cfg.py` and `qmark.py`.
2. **The server's accounting file `/var/log/tac_plus.acct`** -- the primary instrument here,
   read with **`tools/filewin.py`**: mark the byte offset before each leg, read exactly the
   delta afterwards.  **Every "no record" below is a measured zero** (`new-bytes=0`), not an
   absence of output.  The file was `size=0` at the start of the group, so every byte in it
   was produced by this group.
3. **The wire** -- `tools/tb capture tb277 cktac eth3 <secs> <out>.pcap tcp port 49` decoded
   with `tools/tcpflow.py --port 49`, used to confirm that a missing record means **nothing
   was sent**, rather than something being sent and dropped by the server.

VTY sessions are driven with `tools/netlogin.py` over SSH, which opens and closes a session
cleanly -- exactly the stimulus login accounting is specified against.

## STEP 1 (designed) -- baseline OFF, with the server already configured

Purpose: the sharpest possible OFF baseline.  T33411 already showed that with **nothing**
configured a login produces no TCP/49 traffic.  Here the TACACS+ server is configured and
reachable, and only the accounting configuration is absent, so anything measured later is
attributable to the accounting commands alone.

    awplus(config)# tacacs-server host 10.38.22.33 key testing123
    (no `aaa accounting` line of any kind -- `show running-config | include aaa` showed only
     `aaa authentication enable default local` / `aaa authentication login default local`)

    tools/tb tool tb277 cktac filewin /var/log/tac_plus.acct --quiet
      FILEWIN /var/log/tac_plus.acct size=0 ...

    (capture running) netlogin --proto ssh --user manager --password friend --cmd 'show clock'
      LOGIN RESULT: USER  proto=ssh  user=manager  seconds=0.3

    0 packets captured          (tcp port 49, 30 s)
    FILEWIN /var/log/tac_plus.acct size=0 from=0 new-bytes=0 new-lines=0 matched=0

**STEP 1 VERDICT: PASS.**  A complete VTY session -- login, a command, logout -- with a
configured and reachable TACACS+ server produced **zero accounting bytes and zero packets**.
This satisfies **S1599.1.022** in its strongest form and is the baseline every later zero is
measured against.

## STEP 2 (designed) -- enumerate the accounting CLI surface, READ-ONLY

`tools/tb qmark tb277 cktac u2 115200 --mode 'configure terminal' -- '<prefix>'`

| prefix | completions offered |
| --- | --- |
| `aaa accounting ` | `auth-mac` / `auth-web` / **`commands`** Set accounting at specified privilege level / `dot1x` / **`login`** / **`update`** Configure interim accounting update |
| `aaa accounting login ` | `NAME` Named accounting method list / `default` Default accounting method list |
| `aaa accounting login default ` | `none` No accounting record to be sent / `start-stop` / `stop-only` |
| `aaa accounting login default start-stop ` | **`group` only** |
| `aaa accounting login default stop-only ` | **`group` only** |
| `aaa accounting login default none ` | **`group` only** |
| `aaa accounting login default start-stop group ` | `NAME` **"Radius server group name"** / `radius` / `tacacs+` |
| `aaa accounting commands ` | `<1-15>` privilege level range |
| `aaa accounting commands 15 ` | `default` only |
| `aaa accounting commands 15 default ` | **`stop-only` ONLY** |
| `aaa accounting commands 15 default start-stop ` | `% Unrecognized command` |
| `aaa accounting update ` | `periodic` Send accounting records periodically / `<cr>` |
| `aaa accounting update periodic ` | `<1-65535>` Intervals to send accounting update (**in minutes**) |

Three traps worth keeping:

1. ⚠️ **`none` is not a terminal keyword.**  It reads like one ("No accounting record to be
   sent") but `aaa accounting login default none` is rejected with **`% Incomplete command.`**
   -- it still requires `group tacacs+`.  The tester hit this for real before probing it.
2. **Command accounting has no `start-stop`**, only `stop-only`, which is correct for a point
   event but differs from login accounting and is not obvious from the TFS.
3. ⚠️ **A CLI help defect:** `aaa accounting login default start-stop group ?` describes
   `NAME` as **"Radius server group name"**, although the same position accepts `tacacs+`.
   Cosmetic, but it is wrong text on a TACACS+ path.

**STEP 2 VERDICT: PASS.**  Every command this case sends exists; `none`'s real form was
learned here rather than by failing a later step.

## STEP 3 (designed) -- `start-stop` login accounting: the paired records

    awplus(config)# aaa accounting login default start-stop group tacacs+

**`<id>/swi_d.cfg` was saved at this point** (2026-10-20 14:15 NZDT), after the case's setup
and before the measurement steps.

    netlogin --proto ssh --user manager --password friend --cmd 'show clock'

`/var/log/tac_plus.acct`, window `--from 0`, 2 new lines (tabs shown as `|`):

    Oct 20 14:15:09 | 10.38.22.34 | manager | ssh | 10.38.22.33 | start |  | start_time=1792458895 | task_id=1 | service=shell | timezone=UTC
    Oct 20 14:15:09 | 10.38.22.34 | manager | ssh | 10.38.22.33 | stop  |  | stop_time=1792458895  | task_id=1 | service=shell | timezone=UTC | disc-cause=9 | disc-cause-ext=2 | pre-session-time=0 | elapsed_time=0

Wire, 24 packets, **2 flows -- one TCP connection per record**:

    flow ...:49 <-> ...:48542  packets=12  ESTABLISHED+DATA  out 102 B / in 17 B   <- the START
    flow ...:49 <-> ...:48558  packets=12  ESTABLISHED+DATA  out 165 B / in 17 B   <- the STOP

**STEP 3 VERDICT: PASS -- S1599.1.023, S1599.1.024 and S1599.1.028.**  A START on login and a
STOP on logout, both carrying `task_id=1`, so the pair is attributable to one session.  The
STOP is 63 B larger because it adds `stop_time`, `disc-cause`, `disc-cause-ext`,
`pre-session-time` and `elapsed_time`.  The server acknowledges each with 17 B.

Note the user: **`manager`, authenticated by the LOCAL database** (this case never points the
login method list at TACACS+).  **Accounting is therefore proven independent of the
authentication method**, exactly as the TFS states.

## STEP 4 (designed) -- `task_id` increments, and `elapsed_time` is real

A second session, deliberately held open with an 80-packet ping:

    netlogin ... --cmd 'ping 10.38.22.33 repeat 5'       (5 packets ... time 4102ms)

    Oct 20 14:15:58 ... start | start_time=1792458944 | task_id=2 ...
    Oct 20 14:16:02 ... stop  | stop_time=1792458948  | task_id=2 ... elapsed_time=4

**STEP 4 VERDICT: PASS -- S1599.1.027.**  `task_id` advanced 1 -> 2 for the next session,
START and STOP again agree, `stop_time - start_time = 4`, and `elapsed_time=4` matches the
4.1 s the session was actually open.  The accounting record's duration is measured, not
nominal.

## STEP 5 (designed) -- S1599.1.026: a session that predates the configuration

Purpose: the TFS says *"Users already logged in, when changes are made, will retain the
configuration that existed when they logged in."*  The console session had been logged in
since **before** accounting was configured, so logging it out must produce **no STOP**.

`conlogin.py` logs the console out before it logs back in, so one call tests this and the
console's own accounting in the same window.

    show users (before) -> con 0  manager  ttyS0   (session opened ~13:5x, accounting enabled 14:15)

    tools/tb conlogin tb277 cktac u2 115200 --user manager --password friend --logout --expect user
      LOGIN RESULT: USER  user=manager  seconds=0.4  -- MATCH
      conlogin: logged out again
      conlogin: restore login as manager -> USER

Window: exactly **three** records.

    Oct 20 14:16:34 ... manager | ttyS0 | unknown | start | start_time=1792458980 | task_id=3 ...
    Oct 20 14:16:34 ... manager | ttyS0 | unknown | stop  | stop_time=1792458980  | task_id=3 ... elapsed_time=0
    Oct 20 14:16:36 ... manager | ttyS0 | unknown | start | start_time=1792458982 | task_id=4 ...

**STEP 5 VERDICT: PASS, and it is a DIFFERENCE from RADIUS.**

* task_id 3 = the test login and its `--logout`; task_id 4 = the restore login, still open.
* **There is NO stop record for the pre-existing console session.**  It logged out at the
  start of this sequence and produced nothing, because accounting was not configured when it
  logged in.  **S1599.1.026 is satisfied.**
* `platforms/x960.md` §7E.4 records the opposite for RADIUS on this same DUT: *"A session
  opened before accounting was enabled still produces a Stop."*  **TACACS+ and RADIUS behave
  differently here**, and TACACS+ is the one that matches its own specification.

## STEP 6 (designed) -- S1599.1.025: the console and a VTY session are accounted alike

Comparing STEP 3 (VTY) with STEP 5 (console), same configuration, same user:

| field | VTY (ssh) | console |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `port` | `ssh` | `ttyS0` |
| `rem_addr` | `10.38.22.33` | `unknown` |
| record shape | `start` + `stop`, matching `task_id` | `start` + `stop`, matching `task_id` |
| AV pairs | identical set | identical set |

**STEP 6 VERDICT: PASS -- S1599.1.025.**  Login accounting is global: it is not bound to a
line, and both access paths produce the same records.  The only difference is how the session
is identified (`port` / `rem_addr`), which is what those attributes are for.
**Remember this result** -- STEP 10 shows that **command** accounting does NOT share it.

## STEP 7 (designed) -- `stop-only`

    awplus(config)# no aaa accounting login default
    awplus(config)# aaa accounting login default stop-only group tacacs+

    netlogin --proto ssh --user manager --password friend --cmd 'show clock'

    FILEWIN ... new-lines=1
    Oct 20 14:17:06 ... manager | ssh | 10.38.22.33 | stop | stop_time=1792459013 | task_id=5 | ... elapsed_time=0

**STEP 7 VERDICT: PASS.**  Exactly ONE record, a `stop`, with **no preceding `start`**.  It
also confirms the parenthetical in **S1599.1.027** -- *"when stop-only is configured, the
session that closes first uses the first available task_id"*: `task_id=5` was allocated at
the STOP, not at the login.

## STEP 8 (designed) -- `none`, and the CLI trap in it

    awplus(config)# aaa accounting login default none
    % Incomplete command.                       <- see STEP 2, trap 1

    awplus(config)# aaa accounting login default none group tacacs+
    awplus# show running-config | include aaa accounting
    aaa accounting login default none group tacacs+

    (capture running) netlogin --proto ssh --user manager --password friend --cmd 'show clock'

    0 packets captured              (the pcap contains 0 packets; `tcpflow` agrees)
    FILEWIN ... new-bytes=0 new-lines=0

**STEP 8 VERDICT: PASS -- S1599.1.022.**  With the method list explicitly set to `none`, a
full VTY session produced **no record and no packet**.  `none` is a real configured state, not
merely the absence of configuration, and it is silent on both instruments.

⚠️ Reading note: `tcpdump` printed `0 packets captured` but `1 packet received by filter`.
`received by filter` is a kernel-side counter that can exceed the captured count at teardown;
the **pcap itself contains 0 packets** and `tcpflow.py` reads 0 flows, so the measurement is
zero.  Recorded because a raw "1" in a summary line is exactly the kind of number that becomes
a false finding.

## STEP 9 (designed) -- command accounting: what is recorded, and exactly how

    awplus(config)# aaa accounting commands 15 default stop-only group tacacs+

First with privilege **15 only** configured, three exec commands were sent:

    show clock | sh ver | show nosuchthing

    FILEWIN ... new-lines=2
    ... task_id=6  | priv-lvl=15 | cmd=terminal no monitor
    ... task_id=7  | priv-lvl=15 | cmd=terminal no monitor

**None of the three test commands was recorded**, and the only two records are the console
driver's own privilege-15 `terminal no monitor`.  `show clock` and `show version` are
**privilege-1** commands, so with only level 15 configured they are correctly out of scope.
**S1599.1.031 confirmed: the privilege that selects a record is the COMMAND's, not the
session's** -- all three commands were typed in a privilege-15 session.

(The driver's own commands appearing in the records is a measurement artefact of using a
console tool, not a product behaviour.  It is noted so the counts below read correctly.)

Privilege 1 was then added, and the batch repeated with two config-mode commands:

    awplus(config)# aaa accounting commands 1 default stop-only group tacacs+

    show clock | sh ver | show nosuchthing | configure terminal | do sh clock | end

    FILEWIN ... new-lines=6
    task_id=13 | priv-lvl=1  | cmd=terminal length 0          <- the driver's
    task_id=14 | priv-lvl=15 | cmd=terminal no monitor        <- the driver's
    task_id=15 | priv-lvl=1  | cmd=show clock
    task_id=16 | priv-lvl=1  | cmd=show version
    task_id=17 | priv-lvl=15 | cmd=configure terminal
    task_id=18 | priv-lvl=1  | cmd=do sh clock

Read against the TFS, line by line:

| spec | what it requires | measured here |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **S1599.1.033** | a record for an accepted, executed command at the configured privilege | `cmd=show clock` -- **PASS** |
| **S1599.1.036** | the **full expanded** command in the record, not the abbreviation typed | `sh ver` was typed; the record reads **`cmd=show version`** -- **PASS** |
| **S1599.1.034** | no resultant output in the record | `show version` prints ~20 lines; the record carries only `cmd=show version` -- **PASS** |
| **S1599.1.041** | config-mode commands accounted as privilege 15 | `configure terminal` -> `priv-lvl=15`; and in a separate run `ip domain-lookup` typed inside config mode -> `priv-lvl=15` -- **PASS** |
| **S1599.1.042** | a `do` command accounted at privilege **1** and **not** expanded | `do sh clock` -> `priv-lvl=1`, `cmd=do sh clock` -- **both halves PASS**, and note the contrast with `sh ver` three lines above, which WAS expanded |
| **S1599.1.043** | a unique `task_id` per command | 13, 14, 15, 16, 17, 18 -- consecutive, one per command -- **PASS** |
| **S1599.2.08** | **no** record for an invalid command | `show nosuchthing` produced **no record** -- **PASS** |

A separate, focused run confirmed the config-mode half on its own
(`configure terminal` | `ip domain-lookup` | `end` | `show clock`):

    task_id=22 | priv-lvl=15 | cmd=configure terminal
    task_id=23 | priv-lvl=15 | cmd=ip domain-lookup
    task_id=24 | priv-lvl=1  | cmd=show clock

**STEP 9 VERDICT: PASS.**  Seven specifications satisfied, each on its own record.  The
expansion rule and its `do` exception are the two most easily got wrong and both are exactly
right.

### 🔥 FINDING 1 -- `end` leaves configuration mode without an accounting record, while `exit` does not

In the batch above, `end` sits between `do sh clock` (task_id 18) and the next command, and
**there is no record for it**.  The task_ids either side are consecutive, so no record was
lost in transit -- none was ever produced.  Confirmed in the separate focused run as well:
`configure terminal` (22) and `ip domain-lookup` (23) are recorded, `end` is not, and the next
exec command takes 24.

The control that makes this a finding rather than a design choice:

    show clock | configure terminal | exit | show clock      (same configuration, same session)

    task_id=28 | priv-lvl=15 | cmd=configure terminal
    task_id=29 | priv-lvl=15 | cmd=exit              <<<< RECORDED
    task_id=30 | priv-lvl=1  | cmd=show clock

**`exit` and `end` do the same thing -- leave configuration mode -- and only `exit` is
accounted.**

Against the TFS: S1599.1.041 says *"all commands entered after 'configure terminal'"* are
accounted at privilege 15, and `end` is such a command; it is accepted, syntactically correct
and executed (S1599.1.033's three conditions).  The "will not be implemented" list
(S1599.2.06 - .11) names `enable`, an incomplete command, `?`, an invalid command, a command
that did not execute, a boot-configuration command and the `edit` outcome -- **it does not name
`end`**.

Severity: low but real.  A command-accounting audit trail shows an operator entering
configuration mode and what they typed, but -- when they used `end` -- not that they left it.
The next exec-mode record implies the exit, so nothing is unrecoverable; it is an
inconsistency between two synonyms, and the inconsistency is the part worth fixing.

### 🔴🔥 FINDING 2 -- command accounting records CONSOLE commands only; VTY commands are not accounted at all

This is the significant one.  With `aaa accounting commands 1 default stop-only group tacacs+`
**and** `aaa accounting commands 15 default stop-only group tacacs+` both configured and
proven working from the console (STEP 9 above), a VTY session was opened **after** that
configuration and driven through six commands:

    netlogin --proto ssh --host 10.38.22.34 --user manager --password friend
        --cmd 'show clock' --cmd 'sh ver' --cmd 'enable'
        --cmd 'configure terminal' --cmd 'ip domain-lookup' --cmd 'end'

    LOGIN RESULT: USER  proto=ssh  user=manager  seconds=0.4

Every command really ran -- the transcript shows `enable` reaching `awplus#` and
`show clock` returning `UTC Time: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 01:21:28 +0000`.  The result on both
instruments, over a 30 s capture covering the whole session:

    0 packets captured                                      (tcp port 49)
    FILEWIN /var/log/tac_plus.acct ... new-bytes=0 new-lines=0 matched=0

**Zero records and zero packets.**  Run twice, with the same outcome both times; the first
run was a four-command session and the second the six-command session above.

**This is not a missing binding.**  `line vty 0 7` was probed read-only for one:

    awplus(config-line)# accounting ?
      login  Set accounting method list for login session      <- login only; no `commands`
    awplus(config-line)# ?
      accounting / authorization / do / exec-timeout / exit / help / history /
      length / login / no / privilege / show

There is **no per-line command-accounting option anywhere**, which is consistent with the TFS
(*"Command accounting will be globally configured"*, S1599.1.031) -- and global is precisely
what it is not.

**The control is in the same file.**  Login accounting on the *same* VTY sessions works
perfectly (STEPS 3, 4, 7: `port=ssh`, `rem_addr=10.38.22.33`, paired task_ids), and command
accounting on the *console* works perfectly (STEP 9).  So the server, the key, the network
path, the method list and the TACACS+ client are all demonstrably functional in both
dimensions; only the intersection -- **commands on a VTY** -- is silent.

Against the TFS:
* **S1599.1.031** -- *"Command accounting will be globally configured."*  It is configured
  globally and does not apply globally.
* **S1599.1.065** -- *"a maximum of 33 concurrent authentication and **33 concurrent command
  accounting sessions for vty connections**"*.  The specification explicitly dimensions VTY
  command accounting; this build supports zero.
* TPS row 275 says TACACS+ Accounting is **Supported: Yes at Priority 1**, so this is a
  **FINDING, not a platform limit**.

Operational consequence: an operator who administers this switch over SSH -- the normal case
-- leaves **no command audit trail at all**, while the TACACS+ configuration gives every
appearance of recording one.  Silent absence of an audit trail is worse than an obviously
absent one.  **This needs a developer.**

### STEP 9 controls that passed

* **S1599.2.06** -- `enable` produced no record (measured on the VTY session, where it is
  confounded by FINDING 2, and not separately measurable on the console because the console
  session was already privileged; reported as **partially measured** rather than claimed).
* **S1599.2.07** -- `?` produced no record.  `qmark.py` types `PREFIX ?` with no CR and then
  Ctrl-U, and its own self-test proves nothing is executed; the accounting file gained nothing
  across all of this case's `qmark` probes.

## STEP 10 (designed) -- S1599.1.045 / .046: triggers and command accounting are mutually exclusive

Both directions were tested, each from a clean state.

**.046 -- a trigger refused while command accounting is on:**

    awplus# configure terminal
    awplus(config)# trigger 1
    % Triggers cannot be configured while AAA command accounting is enabled.
    awplus# show trigger
    % No triggers currently defined                 <- nothing was created

**.045 -- command accounting refused while a trigger exists:**

    awplus(config)# no aaa accounting commands 15 default
    awplus(config)# no aaa accounting commands 1 default
    awplus(config)# trigger 1
    awplus(config-trigger)# type periodic 1440
    awplus# show trigger
    001 Periodic (1440 ...                      Y  N  Y Continuous  0   smtwtfs

    awplus(config)# aaa accounting commands 15 default stop-only group tacacs+
    % Command accounting cannot be configured while triggers are configured.

**STEP 10 VERDICT: PASS -- both halves, each with its own distinct and accurate error
message**, exactly as S1599.1.045 and S1599.1.046 require ("An error message is printed if a
user tries to ...").  `type periodic 1440` was chosen for the trigger because it touches no
port and no hardware; it was removed again immediately (`no trigger 1`, verified by
`show trigger` -> `% No triggers currently defined` at teardown).

## STEP 11 (designed) -- periodic accounting updates (S1599.1.050 / .052 / .054)

    awplus(config)# no aaa accounting login default
    awplus(config)# aaa accounting login default start-stop group tacacs+
    awplus(config)# aaa accounting update periodic 1          (1 minute, the minimum)

**Observation A** -- a VTY session held open for 81 s:

    Oct 20 14:25:14 | manager | ssh   | 10.38.22.33 | start  | start_time=1792459500 | task_id=44
    Oct 20 14:26:02 | manager | ttyS0 | unknown     | update | task_id=4
    Oct 20 14:26:12 | manager | pts/0 | unknown     | update | task_id=45
    Oct 20 14:26:35 | manager | ssh   | 10.38.22.33 | stop   | stop_time=1792459581  | task_id=44 ... elapsed_time=81

**Observation B** -- a second VTY session held open for 142 s, i.e. two intervals:

    Oct 20 14:27:06 | manager | ssh   | 10.38.22.33 | start  | start_time=1792459612 | task_id=46
    Oct 20 14:28:02 | manager | pts/0 | unknown     | update | task_id=45
    Oct 20 14:28:02 | manager | ttyS0 | unknown     | update | task_id=4
    Oct 20 14:29:02 | manager | ttyS0 | unknown     | update | task_id=4
    Oct 20 14:29:02 | manager | pts/0 | unknown     | update | task_id=45
    Oct 20 14:29:28 | manager | ssh   | 10.38.22.33 | stop   | stop_time=1792459754  | task_id=46 ... elapsed_time=142

**Observation C** -- the control: `show users` showed only `con 0`, and with **no VTY session
open** for 75 s:

    Oct 20 14:30:02 | manager | ttyS0 | unknown | update | task_id=4
    Oct 20 14:31:02 | manager | ttyS0 | unknown | update | task_id=4

-- the `pts/0` updates stop.  So they are bound to a live VTY session; they are not an
orphaned timer.

**What PASSES:** periodic updates are emitted, at exactly the configured 1-minute cadence
(14:26:02, 14:28:02, 14:29:02, 14:30:02, 14:31:02 -- on the minute), only while login
accounting is enabled (**S1599.1.054**), and the **console** session's update carries
`task_id=4`, which is precisely its own START's task_id -- **S1599.1.052 satisfied for the
console**.

### 🔴🔥 FINDING 3 -- a VTY session's periodic update cannot be attributed to that session

S1599.1.052: *"Periodic update messages must contain an AVP `task_id` that is **identical to
the one used by login accounting START and STOP messages for the same session**."*

| session | START / STOP | its periodic update |
| --- | --- | --- |
| console | `port=ttyS0`, `task_id=4` | `port=ttyS0`, **`task_id=4`** -- correct |
| VTY #1 | `port=ssh`, `rem_addr=10.38.22.33`, `task_id=44` | `port=pts/0`, `rem_addr=unknown`, **`task_id=45`** |
| VTY #2 | `port=ssh`, `rem_addr=10.38.22.33`, `task_id=46` | `port=pts/0`, `rem_addr=unknown`, **`task_id=45` again** |

Two independent defects in one record:

1. **The `task_id` is wrong.**  VTY #1's update took `45`, the next free number from the
   global counter, rather than its session's `44`.
2. **It is then REUSED.**  VTY #2 -- a different session, opened after VTY #1 had closed, with
   its own START/STOP pair at `task_id=46` -- emitted updates carrying **the same `45`**.  So
   the update identity is allocated once and shared by successive VTY sessions, which means
   two consecutive sessions' updates are **indistinguishable from each other** as well as
   unattributable to their own session.

Additionally the `port` attribute disagrees with the session's own records (`pts/0` in the
update, `ssh` in START and STOP) and `rem_addr` is lost (`unknown` instead of the client's
address), so **not one of the three fields a server could correlate on matches**.

**S1599.1.053's escape clause does not apply.**  It permits a fresh task_id only when login
accounting was "not active yet (login accounting and periodic updates configured for current
session without re-login)".  Both VTY sessions logged in **after** both commands were
configured, and each had already sent its own START.

Consequence for a real deployment: a TACACS+ accounting server receiving these records can
reconstruct console sessions completely, but for VTY sessions it sees interim updates it
cannot join to any session -- and if it keys on `task_id`, it will merge successive
administrators' updates into one record.  **This needs a developer**, and it is the second
VTY-specific accounting defect in this case.

